--TEST--
Spec § 4: `Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at` bounds each of its four fields, and every bound is named on both sides — hour 23 against 24, minute 59 against 60, second 59 against 60 and nanoseconds 999,999,999 against 1,000,000,000 — with the refusal naming the whole reading it was handed rather than the field that spoiled it
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 4: `at` is the only way to write a `TimeOfDay`'s four fields, so the four
// bounds it enforces are the whole of what a reading can be. Each is asserted
// on both sides here: a member that stopped one entry early would print
// plausibly against either half alone, and a member that clamped instead of
// refusing would print plausibly against the accepted half.
class Clock {
    public static function render(Core\Time\TimeOfDay $t): string {
        return $t->format("HH:mm:ss.SSSSSSSSS");
    }
}

// The accepted side, one field at a time. Each row puts a single field at its
// ceiling and the other three at their floor, so a bound read off the wrong
// field shows up as a rendering rather than as a missing refusal.
echo "hour=", Clock::render(Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(23, 0)), "|\n";
echo "minute=", Clock::render(Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 59)), "|\n";
echo "second=", Clock::render(Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 0, {second: 59})), "|\n";
echo "nanos=", Clock::render(Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 0, {nanos: 999999999})), "|\n";
// All four at once is the dial's last reading, which is where the four bounds
// are simultaneously reachable — so they are not four separate claims about
// four separate readings.
echo "all-four=", Clock::render(
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(23, 59, {second: 59, nanos: 999999999}),
), "|\n";

// The refused side: one past each ceiling, in the same shape. The floors need
// no refusal here and never reach the member — `at`'s parameters and its two
// options are `uint`, so `at(-1, 0)` is `E0401: expected `uint`, found `int``
// in the checker. Where the low end is enforced is the point; a case that must
// fail to compile is a different file.
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(24, 0);
} catch (RuntimeError $hour) {
    echo "hour-24=", $hour->message, "|\n";
}
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 60);
} catch (RuntimeError $minute) {
    echo "minute-60=", $minute->message, "|\n";
}
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 0, {second: 60});
} catch (RuntimeError $second) {
    echo "second-60=", $second->message, "|\n";
}
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 0, {nanos: 1000000000});
} catch (RuntimeError $nanos) {
    echo "nanos-1e9=", $nanos->message, "|\n";
}

// What a refusal says. The four fields arrive together and are refused
// together: the sentence names the whole reading, so a call with two fields
// past their ceilings is one message naming both rather than a report on
// whichever field was checked first. That is why the message reads as a
// reading and not as a parameter — the sibling `with` bounds each option on
// its own and says so, which is a different sentence for a different member.
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(24, 60, {second: 60, nanos: 1000000000});
} catch (RuntimeError $all) {
    echo "all-four-past=", $all->message, "|\n";
}
// A field far past its ceiling is refused by the same sentence rather than
// taken modulo anything: 25 o'clock is not 1am here, which is the difference
// between a constructor and the step `plus` takes.
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(25, 0);
} catch (RuntimeError $far) {
    echo "hour-25=", $far->message, "|\n";
}
try {
    Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(0, 1440);
} catch (RuntimeError $day) {
    echo "minute-1440=", $day->message, "|\n";
}
--EXPECT--
hour=23:00:00.000000000|
minute=00:59:00.000000000|
second=00:00:59.000000000|
nanos=00:00:00.999999999|
all-four=23:59:59.999999999|
hour-24=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `24:0:0.0` is not a time of day|
minute-60=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `0:60:0.0` is not a time of day|
second-60=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `0:0:60.0` is not a time of day|
nanos-1e9=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `0:0:0.1000000000` is not a time of day|
all-four-past=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `24:60:60.1000000000` is not a time of day|
hour-25=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `25:0:0.0` is not a time of day|
minute-1440=Core\Time\TimeOfDay::at(): `0:1440:0.0` is not a time of day|
